+# Why every field is a pill — and the real fix
+
+## The mechanism: `--radius-field` is a theme token, not a one-off value
+
+Scaffold-ETH 2 uses **Tailwind v4 + DaisyUI 5**. In DaisyUI 5 the shape of
+components is driven by three theme-level radius tokens, set inside each
+`@plugin "daisyui/theme"` block:
+
+- `--radius-selector` → selector-ish components (checkbox, toggle, badge…)
+- `--radius-field` → **field components: `input`, `select`, `textarea`, `btn`, tabs, file-input, join items…**
+- `--radius-box` → box components (card, modal, alert…)
+
+DaisyUI's own component CSS does not hardcode a radius. Each field class reads
+the token, roughly:
+
+```css
+.input, .select, .textarea {
+  border-radius: var(--radius-field);
+}
+```
+
+So `--radius-field` is the *single source of truth* for the corner radius of
+every field in the app. Setting it once in the theme:
+
+```css
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "light";
+  --radius-field: 9999rem;   /* <-- this */
+}
+```
+
+means every `.input` / `.select` / `.textarea` DaisyUI renders — existing ones
+and every field anyone adds later — resolves its `border-radius` to `9999rem`.
+
+**Why `9999rem` → pill, and why textareas clip.** A border-radius larger than
+half the box's smaller dimension is clamped by the browser to exactly that
+half. On a single-line input that half-height clamp produces the fully-rounded
+"stadium"/pill ends. On a multi-line textarea the corner arcs have radius ≈
+half the height, so the curves cut deep into the top and bottom corners; the
+textarea's normal padding doesn't reserve room for that curve, so the first/last
+lines of text run under the rounded corners and get clipped.
+
+## Why the per-component `rounded-*` patching keeps losing
+
+The teammate's fix was to add `rounded-lg` on two specific inputs. On those two
+elements it *works* (Tailwind's `utilities` layer overrides DaisyUI's
+`components` layer, so `rounded-lg` = `0.5rem` wins on that element). But it
+loses the war for two structural reasons:
+
+1. **It treats symptoms, not the cause.** The token is still `9999rem`, so it
+   remains the default for the component. Every *new* field starts pill-shaped;
+   you'd have to remember to hand-patch each one forever. The default keeps
+   re-asserting — you can never catch up.
+
+2. **It hardcodes a value disconnected from the theme.** `rounded-lg` bakes in a
+   literal `0.5rem` that no longer tracks the theme token. The patched fields and
+   the theme's intended radius drift apart: change the theme later and the
+   patched ones don't follow; tweak `rounded-lg` and only those two move. That's
+   the "drift out of style with the rest" you're seeing.
+
+Fighting a theme-level token with per-element utilities is inherently
+whack-a-mole. Fix the token and the whole class of fields corrects at once — no
+per-field overrides, nothing to keep in sync.
+
+## The correct fix
+
+**Set `--radius-field` to a sane, rectangular value at the source — in every
+theme block.**
+
+Recommended value: `0.5rem` (this equals `rounded-lg`, so it matches the two
+already-patched inputs — nothing else has to change to stay consistent — and it
+is DaisyUI's own default field radius). Pick a smaller value like `0.375rem`
+if you want tighter corners; the point is it must be a small fixed radius, not
+`9999rem`.
+
+**Every place it must be applied:** DaisyUI resolves theme tokens *per theme
+block*, and Scaffold-ETH 2's `globals.css` defines **two** of them — `light`
+and `dark`. The variable must be set in **both**, or one mode stays wrong
+(a value defined only under `light` does not carry over to `dark`).
+
+```css
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "light";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "dark";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* set/fix here too */
+  ...
+}
+```
+
+Then **delete the `rounded-lg` overrides** on the two patched inputs — they're
+now redundant and are exactly what causes future drift.
+
+### Notes
+- If either theme block currently *omits* `--radius-field`, add it — don't rely
+  on inheritance from the other block.
+- The sibling tokens `--radius-selector` and `--radius-box` govern
+  badges/checkboxes and cards/modals respectively. If those are also set to
+  `9999rem` you'll see the same pill/clip effect on those component families;
+  normalize them the same way (in both theme blocks) if so. For the fields
+  described here, only `--radius-field` matters.

